3.8 Putting it together
Each step above modified hartford and saved it back. We can write that entire chain as one pipeline:

hartford &lt;-
  covid_data |&gt;
  filter(Town == "Hartford") |&gt;
  select(
    date = `Last update date`,
    town = Town,
    total_cases = `Total cases`,
    total_deaths = `Total deaths`
  ) |&gt;
  mutate(date = parse_date(date, format = "%m/%d/%Y")) |&gt;
  arrange(date) |&gt;
  mutate(new_cases = total_cases - lag(total_cases))

5.5 Step 4 — Daily new cases, for every town
Chapter 1’s caveat about “as reported” data applies here at full scale. total_cases is cumulative, so a daily new-case number means computing total_cases - lag(total_cases) again — but this time across 169 towns at once, which means grouping by town first. Without the group_by(), lag() would subtract the last row of one town from the first row of the next.

covid_clean &lt;-
  covid_clean |&gt;
  arrange(town, last_update_date) |&gt;
  group_by(town) |&gt;
  mutate(new_cases = total_cases - lag(total_cases)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

Same reporting caveat as before applies here too — a revised total can still make new_cases negative for a town on a given day. That’s the data, not a bug in this pipeline.

5.7 Step 6 — Generate a one-line summary per town with glue
This is the payoff. Instead of a Commissioner asking “what about Stamford?” and someone re-running a filter by hand, every town already has a ready-made sentence.

town_lines &lt;-
  this_week |&gt;
  mutate(
    line = glue(
      "{town}: {total_cases} total cases ({new_cases} new), {total_deaths} deaths"
    )
  ) |&gt;
  select(town, line)

town_lines |&gt;
  filter(town %in% c("Hartford", "New Haven", "Bridgeport")) |&gt;
  pull(line)

Bridgeport: 42752 total cases (27 new), 450 deaths
Hartford: 33844 total cases (22 new), 417 deaths
New Haven: 35872 total cases (24 new), 289 deaths

5.9 Putting it together
Each step above modified covid_clean and saved it back. Written as one pipeline, the whole cleaning pass is:

covid_clean &lt;-
  covid_data |&gt;
  clean_names() |&gt;
  mutate(
    last_update_date = mdy(last_update_date),
    town = town |&gt; str_trim() |&gt; str_to_title()
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(town, last_update_date) |&gt;
  group_by(town) |&gt;
  mutate(new_cases = total_cases - lag(total_cases)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

11.1 Setup
Chapter 2 left you with covid_clean — tidy snake_case names, real Date values, trimmed and title-cased town names. Time to put that to work. The Commissioner’s briefing packet needs two things this week: a trend chart comparing Hartford to the rest of the state, and a statewide map. Both need to end up as files in outputs/figures/, ready to paste into a slide deck.
library(dplyr)
library(readr)
library(here)
library(janitor)
library(stringr)
library(lubridate)
library(ggplot2)
library(sf)
library(tigris)

covid_data &lt;- read_csv(here("data", "raw", "ct_covid_by_town_2026-06-30.csv"))

covid_clean &lt;-
  covid_data |&gt;
  clean_names() |&gt;
  mutate(
    last_update_date = mdy(last_update_date),
    town = town |&gt; str_trim() |&gt; str_to_title()
  ) |&gt;
  arrange(town, last_update_date) |&gt;
  group_by(town) |&gt;
  mutate(new_cases = total_cases - lag(total_cases)) |&gt;
  ungroup()

15.4 Step 3 — A main() entrypoint
Nothing has actually run yet - so far this script has only defined a constant and a function. main() is where the analysis itself lives: fetch the data, then compute the one number the Commissioner asked for. Wrapping a whole analysis in a function called main() (a convention borrowed from other programming languages) buys you two things: one place to read top-to-bottom to see the entire analysis at a glance, and a script that does nothing until you actually call it. That second part matters once a script gets sourced by other scripts - you don’t want an API call firing off just because something else needed one function out of the file.

main &lt;- function() {
  # main entrypoint into our analysis

  # data ingestion
  data &lt;- fetch_all_data(resource = RESOURCE)

  # key measures
  total_tests &lt;-
    data |&gt;
    mutate(date = ymd_hms(lastupdatedate)) |&gt;
    filter(date == max(date)) |&gt;
    reframe(
      total_pandemic_tests = sum(
        as.numeric(numberoftests),
        na.rm = TRUE
      )
    ) |&gt;
    pull(total_pandemic_tests)

  cat(
    paste(
      "The total number of pandemic tests in these data were:",
      format(total_tests, big.mark = ',')
    )
  )
}
